  "baseline_if_built": "The seat's proposed two-row baseline does not survive re-measurement at prop granularity. ListView.viewType -> 'type': UNREAD, confirmed — plugin-list has zero non-test reads of schema.type and zero of specType, and packages/components/src/renderers/layout/react-page.tsx:93 overwrites type with the block tag, parking an author 'type' as specType, which plugin-list never reads (plugin-charts does, at normalizeChartSchema.ts:277 — so specType has a reader in objectui, just not in this renderer; the seat's claim that specType is unread anywhere is too strong). ListView.objectName -> 'data': the prop IS read (schema.data, ~15 sites), so a prop-level guard is GREEN here and this row would NOT be baselined; only the prescribed shape provider:'object' is unread (zero hits). So a correct prop-level baseline has ONE row, not two, and the row the card names in its own criterion is not in it.",
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "question": "Which evidence source should the reader guard read, given that none of the candidates both resolves in the gate's CI job and carries measured reader facts?",
      "options": [
        "A. A dedicated CI job clones objectui at the pin and the guard greps its sources. Real business need: serves the open window, but the guarded population is rare spec-author edits. Long-term: puts a network clone inside a lint job — a shape this repo's own gate headers decline twice (check-sdui-manifest, check-sdui-lockstep) with stated reasons; attaching it to the existing console job instead inherits that job's cache-conditional dormancy. Anti-AI-error: strong if it runs, since it refuses at publish time. Startup focus: against — a new CI job plus a cross-repo network dependency to fence a window meant to be temporary. Also: triage 5523567239 says a repo-level CI gate outside the generator is domain:devx, i.e. a re-route, not this lane.",
        "B. objectui exports a per-component reads manifest (which schema paths its renderers actually read, including nested shapes like data.object); objectstack vendors it at pin bump exactly as sdui.manifest.json is vendored today. Real business need: the only shape that answers the question actually asked, and it generalises — the pin-citation header prices the current hand-derivation of cross-repo read points at a full dispatch cycle (#9397), so liveness sweeps would reuse it. Long-term: contract-first, and it copies a proven in-repo precedent (checked-in artefact + sha256 record + pin-freshness gate), offline per-PR, survives pin moves. Anti-AI-error: strongest — the declaration is refused unless a measured reader exists, with no consumer-side tolerance. Startup focus: highest upfront cost and cross-lane; justified only while the deprecation window stays open, or if the artefact earns its keep for liveness generally.",
        "C. Build on the checked-in root sdui.manifest.json. Real business need: cheapest, resolves today, no new artefact. Long-term: reuses a pin-anchored artefact, which is attractive, but it is a declaration corpus. Anti-AI-error: actively harmful — measured wrong in both directions above, so it would manufacture a false green on the very row the card cites and a false red elsewhere; a gate that reports 'verified' without verifying is the tolerance shape axis 3 forbids. Startup focus: cheap, but the cheapness buys a wrong answer. NOT RECOMMENDED.",
        "D. Build no reader guard; put the deprecation window itself back to the maintainer. Real business need: the name-validity half is ALREADY gated — packages/spec/src/ui/react-blocks.test.ts:164-165 asserts a replacedBy names a real prop on the block — so the residual exposure is one further unread replacement authored during the window. Long-term: fewest moving parts; the defect class disappears when the window closes. Anti-AI-error: weakest — nothing refuses a new unread replacement. Startup focus: strongest claim, maintainer 2026-08-27 verbatim 「项目在创业阶段,用户也很少,短期不考虑渐进。」 — deprecated spellings default to immediate retirement with no staged window, and #14791's B ruling keeps open exactly the window this guard would be built to fence."
      ],
      "recommendation": "B if a guard is wanted at all — it is the only shape that both resolves offline in the gate's own job and carries measured reader facts, and it is the only one that can express the shape-level fact that the incident actually turned on. But I do not recommend starting B before D's axis-4 question is settled by the maintainer, because B's cross-lane cost is only justified while the window stays open, and axis 4 argues the window should not. Explicitly not C: it resolves, which is what makes it dangerous. A belongs to domain:devx if it is chosen at all."
    },
    {
      "question": "At what granularity should the criterion bind — the replacement PROP, or the replacement SHAPE the deprecation note prescribes?",
      "options": [
        "Prop-level, as the card's executable criterion is literally worded. Measured consequence: green on replacedBy: 'data', because schema.data has ~15 read sites — the guard would not refuse the incident that motivated it.",
        "Shape-level: the guard checks that the spelling the note prescribes (data={{ provider: 'object', object }}) has a reader, i.e. that a provider === 'object' branch exists. Measured consequence: red on both existing rows today, which is the honest answer and matches the incident. Costs more from the evidence source: it needs nested-path reader facts, which only option B can supply."
      ],
      "recommendation": "Shape-level. Prop-level is measurably vacuous for the case on the card, and adopting it would ship a guard whose green means nothing — the same failure the card exists to end. This also raises the bar on the evidence source, which is part of why B is the recommendation above rather than a convenience."
    }
